1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,968
This is ECE 358, video 11. The topic is TCP congestion control. It covers Chapter 3, slides 73 to 89.

2
00:00:12,418 --> 00:00:35,501
After this video, you can explain congestion and the TCP mechanisms that control it. You can apply the Tahoe and Reno rules. You can read a C wind plot round by round. This is the 2016 final question and problem set 3, problem 4. And you can calculate the TCP rate and the average throughput of Reno.

3
00:00:35,951 --> 00:00:47,471
Three senders send data through one router. The router has one output link with rate R. When the input rate is less than R, the output buffer stays almost empty.

4
00:00:47,921 --> 00:00:56,454
When the senders send faster than R, packets wait in the output buffer. The queue grows. So the queueing delay becomes long.

5
00:00:56,904 --> 00:01:04,285
When the buffer is full, the router drops the next packet that arrives. This is packet loss from buffer overflow.

6
00:01:04,735 --> 00:01:10,687
This is congestion: too many sources sending too much data too fast for the network to handle.

7
00:01:11,137 --> 00:01:23,532
The solution is congestion control. The senders notice the congestion and control their data rate. The slide calls it a service to the underlying network, for the common good of all.

8
00:01:23,982 --> 00:01:34,009
Flow control solves a different problem. One sender is too fast for one receiver. The receive buffer fills. The beneficiary is the receiver.

9
00:01:34,459 --> 00:01:45,829
Congestion control solves the problem in the network. Many senders together are too fast for a router and its link. The full buffer is in the router, not in the receiver.

10
00:01:46,279 --> 00:02:00,722
Keep the two apart. Flow control protects the receiver and uses R wind, which the receiver sends. Congestion control protects the network and uses C wind, which the sender keeps. You will see C wind next.

11
00:02:01,172 --> 00:02:17,513
There are two basic approaches. The first is end-to-end congestion control. The network gives no explicit feedback. The sender sees only the data and the ACKs. It infers congestion from the loss and the delay that it observes.

12
00:02:17,963 --> 00:02:20,459
This is the approach that TCP takes.

13
00:02:20,909 --> 00:02:34,541
The second approach is network-assisted congestion control. A congested router gives direct feedback to the hosts whose flows pass through it. The red arrows show this explicit congestion information.

14
00:02:34,991 --> 00:02:48,645
The feedback can give the congestion level, or it can set the sending rate. Examples are TCP E C N, which uses bits in the TCP header, A T M, and the deck bit protocol.

15
00:02:49,095 --> 00:02:54,471
Here is the sender's sequence number space. The green bytes are already acknowledged.

16
00:02:54,921 --> 00:03:05,481
The orange bytes are sent but not yet acknowledged. They are in flight. The difference between last byte sent and last byte acked is the number of bytes in flight.

17
00:03:05,931 --> 00:03:16,917
C wind is the congestion window. It is a window over the sequence number space. The sender can send the blue bytes too, because they are still inside C wind.

18
00:03:17,367 --> 00:03:31,981
So the TCP sender limits its transmission. Last byte sent minus last byte acked must be at most the minimum of C wind and R wind. This equation joins congestion control and flow control.

19
00:03:32,431 --> 00:03:46,191
C wind is kept by the sender. The sender changes it when it sees congestion, that is, triple duplicate ACKs or a timeout. R wind comes from the receiver, in the segment header, for flow control.

20
00:03:46,641 --> 00:03:53,126
How fast does a TCP sender send? Roughly, it sends C wind bytes into the network.

21
00:03:53,576 --> 00:03:58,589
Then it waits about one RTT, until the ACKs for these bytes come back.

22
00:03:59,039 --> 00:04:11,605
Then it sends the next C wind bytes. So the sender sends one window per RTT. The TCP rate is about C wind divided by RTT, in bytes per second.

23
00:04:12,055 --> 00:04:24,535
Here is the example from slide 80. C wind is 20 MSS. One MSS is 1500 bytes. The RTT is 200 ms.

24
00:04:24,985 --> 00:04:40,089
The rate is 20 times 1500 times 8 bits, divided by 0 point 2 seconds. That is 1 point 2 Mbps. So to change the sending rate, TCP changes C wind.

25
00:04:40,539 --> 00:04:57,328
TCP uses AIMD. The approach: increase the sending rate until packet loss occurs, then decrease it. Additive increase: C wind grows by 1 MSS every RTT, until the sender detects a loss.

26
00:04:57,778 --> 00:05:07,037
Multiplicative decrease: at a loss event, the sender cuts C wind, for example in half. Here C wind drops from 14 to 7.

27
00:05:07,487 --> 00:05:17,343
Then additive increase starts again. The result is a sawtooth. The sender probes for the available bandwidth, backs off at a loss, and probes again.

28
00:05:17,793 --> 00:05:33,707
Why AIMD? AIMD is a distributed, asynchronous algorithm. Each sender acts alone. It has been shown to optimize congested flow rates network-wide, and to be stable, with fairness for all flows.

29
00:05:34,157 --> 00:05:46,467
How does a TCP sender detect a loss? There are two mechanisms. The first is triple duplicate ACKs. Host A sends segments 1 to 5. Segment 2 is lost.

30
00:05:46,917 --> 00:06:03,706
Host B acknowledges segment 1 with ACK 2. ACK 2 means: the next segment I expect is segment 2. Segments 3, 4 and 5 arrive out of order. For each one, Host B sends ACK 2 again. These are 3 duplicate ACKs.

31
00:06:04,156 --> 00:06:10,471
After 3 duplicate ACKs, Host A resends segment 2. It does not wait for the timer.

32
00:06:10,921 --> 00:06:20,670
The second mechanism is a timeout. Segment 2 is lost, and no ACK comes back. The timer of segment 2 runs out, and Host A resends it.

33
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:36,693
The reaction depends on the mechanism. On triple duplicate ACKs, Reno cuts C wind in half. On a timeout, Reno and Tahoe cut C wind to 1 MSS. A timeout is the stronger signal of congestion.

34
00:06:37,143 --> 00:06:53,783
A new connection does not know the available bandwidth. So it starts with slow start. At the start, C wind is 1 MSS. S S thresh, the slow start threshold, has an initial value, for example 64 kilobytes.

35
00:06:54,233 --> 00:07:05,924
In round 1, the sender sends one segment. One ACK comes back. For every ACK received, the sender adds 1 MSS to C wind. So C wind becomes 2.

36
00:07:06,374 --> 00:07:13,457
In round 2, the sender sends two segments. Two ACKs come back, so C wind grows by 2, to 4.

37
00:07:13,907 --> 00:07:20,264
In round 3, the sender sends four segments. Four ACKs come back, so C wind becomes 8.

38
00:07:20,714 --> 00:07:35,711
So adding 1 MSS for every ACK doubles C wind every RTT. The initial rate is slow, but it ramps up exponentially fast. Slow start continues until C wind reaches S S thresh.

39
00:07:36,161 --> 00:07:51,009
When does exponential growth stop? It stops when C wind reaches S S thresh. In this example from slide 82, S S thresh is 8. C wind grows 1, 2, 4, 8 in rounds 1 to 4.

40
00:07:51,459 --> 00:08:03,641
At 8, C wind has reached S S thresh. The sender switches to linear growth, the additive increase of AIMD. C wind becomes 9, 10, 11 and 12.

41
00:08:04,091 --> 00:08:08,549
After round 8, at C wind 12, the sender detects a loss.

42
00:08:08,999 --> 00:08:23,805
S S thresh is not a constant. It is a variable. On a loss event, the sender sets S S thresh to half of C wind just before the loss. Here the new S S thresh is 12 divided by 2, which is 6.

43
00:08:24,255 --> 00:08:35,967
Here S S thresh is 4. C wind has reached 4, so the connection is in the congestion avoidance phase, C A. The sender sends 4 segments, and 4 ACKs come back.

44
00:08:36,417 --> 00:08:51,371
In C A, the sender adds 1 MSS to C wind only when all the segments of the previous C wind are acknowledged. So after the 4 ACKs, C wind becomes 5. The sender sends 5 segments.

45
00:08:51,821 --> 00:09:05,709
After those 5 ACKs, C wind becomes 6. So in slow start, C wind doubles every RTT. In congestion avoidance, it grows by only 1 MSS every RTT.

46
00:09:06,159 --> 00:09:22,287
This table is question 47 of the transport part of the question bank. Fill it row by row. TCP Tahoe, triple duplicate ACKs: S S thresh becomes C wind over 2, and C wind becomes 1 MSS.

47
00:09:22,737 --> 00:09:28,049
TCP Tahoe, timeout: the same. Tahoe always sets C wind to 1.

48
00:09:28,499 --> 00:09:37,374
TCP Reno, timeout: also C wind over 2 and 1 MSS. A timeout always goes back to slow start.

49
00:09:37,824 --> 00:09:54,251
TCP Reno, triple duplicate ACKs: this is the one different cell. S S thresh equals C wind over 2, and C wind equals S S thresh. So C wind is cut in half, and Reno stays in congestion avoidance.

50
00:09:54,701 --> 00:10:01,442
Duplicate ACKs show that the network can still deliver some segments. So Reno only halves C wind.

51
00:10:01,892 --> 00:10:08,441
Slide 85 compares Reno and Tahoe. In rounds 1 to 8, both behave the same.

52
00:10:08,891 --> 00:10:19,046
After round 8, at C wind 12, the sender gets triple duplicate ACKs. Both variants set S S thresh to 12 over 2, which is 6.

53
00:10:19,496 --> 00:10:34,280
Tahoe sets C wind to 1 MSS. Slow start gives 1, 2, 4. The next doubling would give 8, but it stops at S S thresh, which is 6. Then C wind grows by 1: 7, 8, 9.

54
00:10:34,730 --> 00:10:44,330
Reno sets C wind to S S thresh, which is 6, in round 9. Then it grows linearly: 7, 8, up to 12 in round 15.

55
00:10:44,780 --> 00:10:53,100
The Reno line is always above the Tahoe line after the loss. So Reno sends more data in rounds 9 to 15.

56
00:10:53,550 --> 00:11:14,030
What is the average throughput of TCP Reno? Ignore slow start, and assume that the sender always has data to send. W is the window size, in bytes, where a loss occurs. Losses come from triple duplicate ACKs only, so C wind drops from W to W over 2.

57
00:11:14,480 --> 00:11:24,016
In each tooth, C wind climbs in a straight line from W over 2 back to W. It grows by 1 MSS per RTT.

58
00:11:24,466 --> 00:11:40,125
The average of a straight line is the middle of its two ends. W over 2 plus W, divided by 2, is three quarters of W. So on average, three quarters of W bytes are in flight in each RTT.

59
00:11:40,575 --> 00:11:58,431
So the average TCP throughput is three quarters times W divided by RTT, in bytes per second. This is the answer to question 49 of the question bank. Question 49 has no timeouts, so every loss gives this sawtooth.

60
00:11:58,881 --> 00:12:16,971
Is there a better way than AIMD to probe for bandwidth? TCP CUBIC uses this idea. W max is the sending rate at which the sender detected a congestion loss. The congestion state of the bottleneck link has probably not changed much since then.

61
00:12:17,421 --> 00:12:27,363
Classic TCP cuts the window to W max over 2. Then it climbs back in a straight line, slowly, all the way to W max.

62
00:12:27,813 --> 00:12:39,866
CUBIC also cuts to W max over 2. But then it ramps up to W max fast at first. Near W max, it slows down and approaches W max carefully.

63
00:12:40,316 --> 00:12:48,615
So CUBIC spends more time near W max. In this example, CUBIC has a higher throughput than classic TCP.

64
00:12:49,065 --> 00:12:58,622
Slide 88 gives the details. K is the point in time when the TCP window will reach W max. K itself is tunable.

65
00:12:59,072 --> 00:13:10,293
CUBIC increases W as a function of the cube of the distance between the current time and K. Far from K, the cube is large, so the increase is large.

66
00:13:10,743 --> 00:13:17,933
Near K, the cube is small, so the increase is small and careful. The curve becomes flat at W max.

67
00:13:18,383 --> 00:13:26,532
After K, the distance grows again, so the window grows faster. CUBIC probes for more bandwidth above W max.

68
00:13:26,982 --> 00:13:37,990
This is the cubic part that question 50 asks for. TCP CUBIC is the default in Linux, and the most popular TCP for popular Web servers.

69
00:13:38,440 --> 00:13:55,037
Now the questions. This plot is from the fall 2016 final, on slide 89. Problem 4 of problem set 3 is the same question. It shows C wind, in segments, for each transmission round of TCP Reno.

70
00:13:55,487 --> 00:14:01,653
There are ten parts, a to j. Read each part, and use the plot. Pause the video and try it.

71
00:14:02,103 --> 00:14:13,260
Parts a and b. Use the shape of the curve. In slow start, C wind doubles each round. In congestion avoidance, C wind grows by 1 each round.

72
00:14:13,710 --> 00:14:30,862
From round 1 to round 6, C wind goes 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32. It doubles. So slow start runs in rounds 1 to 6. From round 23 to 26, C wind goes 1, 2, 4, 8. That is slow start again.

73
00:14:31,312 --> 00:14:50,128
From round 6 to round 16, C wind goes from 32 to 42, one more each round. From round 17 to round 22, it goes from 21 to 26. So congestion avoidance runs in rounds 6 to 16, and 17 to 22.

74
00:14:50,578 --> 00:15:08,114
Part c. After round 16, C wind falls from 42 to 21. That is half. In Reno, only triple duplicate ACKs cut C wind in half. So the loss was detected by a triple duplicate ACK. A timeout would set C wind to 1.

75
00:15:08,564 --> 00:15:16,927
Part d. After round 22, C wind falls from 26 to 1. So the loss was detected by a timeout.

76
00:15:17,377 --> 00:15:29,025
The rule to remember. In Reno, triple duplicate ACKs set C wind to S S thresh, which is half of C wind. A timeout sets C wind to 1 MSS.

77
00:15:29,475 --> 00:15:43,683
Part e. Slow start stops and congestion avoidance begins when C wind reaches S S thresh. That happens at C wind 32, in round 6. So the initial S S thresh is 32.

78
00:15:44,133 --> 00:15:57,509
Part f. The loss after round 16 sets S S thresh to half of C wind just before the loss. That is 42 over 2, which is 21. It stays 21 in round 18.

79
00:15:57,959 --> 00:16:08,433
Part g. The timeout after round 22 sets S S thresh to 26 over 2, which is 13. It stays 13 in round 24.

80
00:16:08,883 --> 00:16:19,934
Check the result. In rounds 23 to 26, C wind goes up to 8, which is less than 13. So the plot is still in slow start, as part a says.

81
00:16:20,384 --> 00:16:26,763
Part h. Each round sends C wind segments. Count them. Round 1 sends segment 1.

82
00:16:27,213 --> 00:16:30,669
Round 2 sends 2 segments, numbers 2 and 3.

83
00:16:31,119 --> 00:16:34,383
Round 3 sends 4 segments, 4 to 7.

84
00:16:34,833 --> 00:16:38,203
Round 4 sends 8 segments, 8 to 15.

85
00:16:38,653 --> 00:16:42,877
Round 5 sends 16 segments, 16 to 31.

86
00:16:43,327 --> 00:16:51,925
Round 6 sends 32 segments, 32 to 63. So rounds 1 to 6 send 63 segments in total.

87
00:16:52,375 --> 00:17:02,956
Round 7 has C wind 33. It sends segments 64 to 96. Segment 70 is in this range. So the answer is round 7.

88
00:17:03,406 --> 00:17:13,689
The problem set solution writes 15 to 31 for round 5. It should be 16 to 31. The answer, round 7, does not change.

89
00:17:14,139 --> 00:17:35,536
Part i. A loss after round 26 is detected by a triple duplicate ACK. Just before the loss, C wind is 8. Reno sets S S thresh to 8 over 2, which is 4, and sets C wind to S S thresh. So C wind is 4 and S S thresh is 4. This matches the answer on the slide.

90
00:17:35,986 --> 00:17:49,618
Part j. Now use Tahoe, with triple duplicate ACKs at round 16. S S thresh becomes 42 over 2, which is 21. But Tahoe always sets C wind to 1 MSS.

91
00:17:50,068 --> 00:18:00,180
Then slow start runs again. C wind is 1 in round 17, 2 in round 18, and 4 in round 19. All are below 21.

92
00:18:00,630 --> 00:18:09,419
So at round 19, S S thresh is 21 and C wind is 4. This matches the slide answer, 21 and 4.

93
00:18:09,869 --> 00:18:27,875
Problem 6 of problem set 3. There is no loss. TCP uses AIMD without slow start. C wind grows by 1 MSS each time a batch of ACKs arrives, that is, once per RTT. Pause the video and try it.

94
00:18:28,325 --> 00:18:45,583
Part a. C wind starts at 1 MSS. After 1 RTT it is 2, after 2 RTTs it is 3, and so on. It reaches 6 MSS after 5 RTTs. So the answer is 5 RTT.

95
00:18:46,033 --> 00:18:54,204
Part b. In each RTT, the sender sends one window. In the first RTT, it sends 1 MSS.

96
00:18:54,654 --> 00:19:01,630
Then it sends 2, 3, 4 and 5. The total up to 5 RTT is 15 MSS.

97
00:19:02,080 --> 00:19:13,387
The average throughput is 15 MSS divided by 5 RTT. That is 3 MSS per RTT. This matches the problem set solution.

98
00:19:13,837 --> 00:19:36,279
The question bank also has descriptive questions. Pause the video and try it. Question 43 asks for the purpose and the mechanism. The purpose: the sender must not send too fast for the network, that is, the routers and links. The mechanism is end-to-end. The sender keeps C wind and changes it when it detects a loss.

99
00:19:36,729 --> 00:19:49,849
Question 44 asks for the equation that joins congestion control and flow control. Last byte sent minus last byte acked is at most the minimum of C wind and R wind.

100
00:19:50,299 --> 00:19:58,214
Question 46 asks how the sender infers a loss. There are two ways: triple duplicate ACKs, and a timeout.

101
00:19:58,664 --> 00:20:13,661
Question 45 asks for the general idea of AIMD. Use the earlier slides: slow start to S S thresh, then congestion avoidance, then the loss reaction from the Tahoe and Reno table.

102
00:20:14,111 --> 00:20:19,359
The question bank has no published answers. These answers use the slides only.

103
00:20:19,809 --> 00:20:35,745
Question 48 asks how the value of S S thresh increases. Pause the video and try it. S S thresh does not grow round by round, as C wind does. It changes only at a loss event. Here it starts at 8.

104
00:20:36,195 --> 00:20:48,846
At a loss, the new S S thresh is half of C wind just before the loss. In this example, the loss occurs at C wind 12, so S S thresh becomes 6. It went down.

105
00:20:49,296 --> 00:21:11,696
Then C wind grows linearly for a long time, up to 22. The next loss sets S S thresh to 22 over 2, which is 11. So S S thresh increases when the loss occurs at a C wind larger than 2 times the old S S thresh. This example is ours. The rule is from slide 82.

106
00:21:12,146 --> 00:21:22,023
Summary. Congestion means delays and losses in the routers. TCP uses end-to-end control, with C wind kept by the sender.

107
00:21:22,473 --> 00:21:30,217
Slow start doubles C wind every RTT. Congestion avoidance adds 1 MSS per RTT.

108
00:21:30,667 --> 00:21:40,630
At a loss, S S thresh becomes half of C wind, and C wind follows the Tahoe and Reno table. The next video starts the network layer.
